# Spec-Driven Development Rules

## Overview

Spec-driven development is a methodology where we create detailed specifications before writing any code. This ensures:
- Clear understanding of requirements
- Consistent implementation
- Easier maintenance
- Better collaboration between team members

## Component Specifications

### Component Spec Template
```jsx
/**
 * Component Specification: UserManagement
 * 
 * Purpose:
 * Provides an interface for administrators to view, create, edit, and delete user accounts.
 * 
 * Props:
 * - initialFilter: Object - Initial filter values for the user list
 * - onUserCreated: Function - Callback when a user is successfully created
 * - onUserUpdated: Function - Callback when a user is successfully updated
 * - onUserDeleted: Function - Callback when a user is successfully deleted
 * 
 * State:
 * - users: Array - List of user objects from the API
 * - isLoading: Boolean - Whether data is being loaded
 * - filters: Object - Current filter values
 * - pagination: Object - Current pagination state
 * - selectedUser: Object|null - Currently selected user for editing
 * - isCreating: Boolean - Whether the create modal is open
 * - isEditing: Boolean - Whether the edit modal is open
 * - isDeleting: Boolean - Whether the delete confirmation is open
 * 
 * API Dependencies:
 * - GET /users - Fetch users with pagination and filtering
 * - POST /users - Create new user
 * - PUT /users/:id - Update existing user
 * - DELETE /users/:id - Delete user
 * 
 * Component Dependencies:
 * - DataTable - For displaying user list
 * - UserForm - For create/edit forms
 * - DeleteConfirmModal - For delete confirmation
 * 
 * Behavior:
 * 1. On mount, load users from API with default filters
 * 2. Support filtering by name, email, role, status
 * 3. Support sorting by any column
 * 4. Pagination with server-side implementation
 * 5. Create user opens modal form, submits to API on save
 * 6. Edit user opens modal form with pre-filled data, submits to API on save
 * 7. Delete user shows confirmation dialog, deletes on confirm
 * 8. All operations show appropriate loading states and error messages
 */
```

### Feature Specification Example
```jsx
/**
 * Feature Specification: Role-Based Access Control
 * 
 * Overview:
 * Implement a comprehensive role-based access control system that restricts
 * access to features based on user roles and permissions.
 * 
 * Components:
 * 1. RoleManagement - UI for managing roles and permissions
 * 2. RoleFunctionalitiesMapping - UI for mapping roles to functionalities
 * 3. PrivateRoute - Route wrapper for access control
 * 
 * Data Models:
 * - Role: { id, name, description, is_active, created_at, updated_at }
 * - Functionality: { id, name, description, module, is_active }
 * - RoleFunctionalityMapping: { role_id, functionality_id, access_level }
 * 
 * API Endpoints:
 * - GET /roles - List all roles
 * - POST /roles - Create role
 * - PUT /roles/:id - Update role
 * - DELETE /roles/:id - Delete role
 * - GET /functionalities - List all functionalities
 * - POST /roles/:id/functionalities - Assign functionalities to role
 * - GET /roles/:id/functionalities - Get functionalities for a role
 * 
 * Implementation Details:
 * 1. Create roles management interface
 * 2. Create functionalities management interface
 * 3. Create role-functionality mapping interface
 * 4. Implement PrivateRoute component that checks user permissions
 * 5. Add permission checks to all actions in the system
 * 6. Update user management to assign roles to users
 * 
 * Testing Criteria:
 * 1. Users can only access routes they have permission for
 * 2. UI elements are conditionally rendered based on permissions
 * 3. API calls are blocked if user lacks permission
 * 4. Admin can create, update, and delete roles
 * 5. Admin can assign functionalities to roles
 */
```

## API Integration Specifications

### API Integration Spec Template
```jsx
/**
 * API Integration Specification: User Management
 * 
 * Endpoints:
 * 
 * 1. GET /users
 *    - Purpose: Retrieve paginated list of users
 *    - Query Parameters:
 *      - page: number (default: 1)
 *      - limit: number (default: 50)
 *      - search: string (optional)
 *      - status: string (optional, 'active' or 'inactive')
 *      - role: string (optional)
 *    - Response: {
 *        data: Array<User>,
 *        meta: {
 *          total: number,
 *          page: number,
 *          limit: number,
 *          totalPages: number
 *        }
 *      }
 *    - Error Handling:
 *      - 401: Redirect to login
 *      - 403: Show permission error
 *      - 500: Show server error message
 * 
 * 2. POST /users
 *    - Purpose: Create new user
 *    - Request Body: {
 *        name: string,
 *        email: string,
 *        role: string,
 *        password: string (optional, system-generated if empty),
 *        status: boolean (default: true)
 *      }
 *    - Response: { 
 *        user: User,
 *        message: string 
 *      }
 *    - Validation:
 *      - name: Required, min 3 chars
 *      - email: Required, valid email format, unique
 *      - role: Required, must exist in system
 *    - Error Handling:
 *      - 400: Show validation errors
 *      - 401: Redirect to login
 *      - 403: Show permission error
 *      - 409: Show conflict error (email already exists)
 *      - 500: Show server error message
 * 
 * 3. PUT /users/:id
 *    - Purpose: Update existing user
 *    - URL Parameters:
 *      - id: string (user ID)
 *    - Request Body: {
 *        name: string (optional),
 *        email: string (optional),
 *        role: string (optional),
 *        status: boolean (optional)
 *      }
 *    - Response: { 
 *        user: User,
 *        message: string 
 *      }
 *    - Validation: Same as POST
 *    - Error Handling: Same as POST, plus:
 *      - 404: Show not found error
 * 
 * 4. DELETE /users/:id
 *    - Purpose: Delete user
 *    - URL Parameters:
 *      - id: string (user ID)
 *    - Response: { 
 *        message: string 
 *      }
 *    - Error Handling: Same as PUT
 */
```

## React Query Hook Specifications

### Query Hook Spec Template
```jsx
/**
 * React Query Hook Specification: useUsers
 * 
 * Purpose:
 * Provide data access and mutation capabilities for user management.
 * 
 * Exports:
 * - useUsers(filters, pagination) - Hook for fetching users list
 * - useUser(id) - Hook for fetching a single user
 * - useCreateUser() - Hook for creating a user
 * - useUpdateUser() - Hook for updating a user
 * - useDeleteUser() - Hook for deleting a user
 * 
 * Query Keys:
 * - users.all: ['users']
 * - users.list: () => [...users.all, 'list'] 
 * - users.detail: (id) => [...users.all, 'detail', id]
 * 
 * Implementation Details:
 * 
 * 1. useUsers(filters = {}, pagination = { page: 1, limit: 50 })
 *    - Returns: { data, isLoading, error, refetch }
 *    - Data structure: { 
 *        data: Array<User>, 
 *        meta: { 
 *          total, page, limit, totalPages 
 *        } 
 *      }
 *    - Error handling: Shows toast error message
 *    - Caching: 5 minutes stale time
 * 
 * 2. useUser(id)
 *    - Returns: { data, isLoading, error, refetch }
 *    - Data structure: User
 *    - Enabled only when id is provided
 *    - Error handling: Shows toast error message
 * 
 * 3. useCreateUser()
 *    - Returns: { mutate, isLoading, error }
 *    - mutate(userData): Promise
 *    - On success: 
 *      - Shows success toast
 *      - Invalidates users list query
 *    - On error: Shows toast error message
 * 
 * 4. useUpdateUser()
 *    - Returns: { mutate, isLoading, error }
 *    - mutate({ id, data }): Promise
 *    - On success:
 *      - Shows success toast
 *      - Invalidates users list query and specific user query
 *    - On error: Shows toast error message
 * 
 * 5. useDeleteUser()
 *    - Returns: { mutate, isLoading, error }
 *    - mutate(id): Promise
 *    - On success:
 *      - Shows success toast
 *      - Invalidates users list query
 *    - On error: Shows toast error message
 * 
 * Usage example:
 * 
 * function UserList() {
 *   const [filters, setFilters] = useState({});
 *   const [pagination, setPagination] = useState({ page: 1, limit: 50 });
 *   const { data, isLoading } = useUsers(filters, pagination);
 *   const { mutate: deleteUser } = useDeleteUser();
 *   
 *   // Component logic...
 * }
 */
```

## Implementation Steps

### 1. Create Feature Specification
Start by creating a comprehensive feature specification document that outlines:
- Purpose and overview
- Data models
- API requirements
- UI components
- Business logic
- Edge cases and error handling

### 2. Review and Approve Specification
Have team members review the specification to ensure:
- Requirements are clear
- Edge cases are covered
- Technical approach is sound
- Implementation is feasible

### 3. Break Down Into Tasks
Divide the specification into smaller, manageable tasks:
- API implementation
- Data models
- UI components
- Integration testing

### 4. Implement Following Specification
Use the specification as a guide during implementation:
- Reference it regularly to ensure all requirements are met
- Update it if requirements change
- Use it as documentation for future reference

### 5. Test Against Specification
Ensure all implementation meets the specification:
- Test all functionality
- Verify edge cases
- Check error handling
- Validate performance requirements

